 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "FINGAL'S CAVE. The cave is about 69 metres deep and the arched roof rises like a cathedral's. The walls are made of columns of basalt, thousands of them, as tidy as organ pipes. Fingal is Fionn mac Cumhaill, the giant, in Scottish clothes. The same giant built the Giant's Causeway, the story goes, to walk across to fight a rival here.",
   "x": 7,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "tob_fingals_cave_inside",
   "title": "Fingal's Cave"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "staffa_gaelic_name",
   "title": "The Melodious Cave",
   "text": "AN UAMH BHINN. That is the Gaelic name for this cave: the Melodious Cave. Staffa itself is Old Norse, from a word for a staff or pillar, so the Vikings named the island for its columns and the Gaels named the cave for its sound."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "staffa_puffins",
   "title": "Staffa's Puffins",
   "text": "PUFFINS. Atlantic puffins nest in burrows in the turf on top of Staffa, from about April to early August, and spend the rest of the year far out at sea. Visitors who land in the season can walk among them, and they mostly ignore the audience."
  }
 ],
